2026-06-30 15:36:34,021 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-5c590b0a-2865-4c2b-83fb-f54c5b26d8bc', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: disease\nsdrf_column: characteristics[disease]\nontology: EFO:0000408, MONDO, DOID, PATO\ndescription: Disease state of the sample.\ninstructions: For healthy/normal samples use "normal" (PATO:0000461). For diseased\n  samples use MONDO or DOID terms. Return null only if completely unstated.\n  Most XL-MS samples are "normal" unless explicitly studying a disease model.\n\nterm: phenotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phenotype]\nontology: EFO:0000651, PATO, EFO\ndescription: Observable characteristics or traits (drug sensitivity, molecular markers,\n  expression phenotypes, environmental responses).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. This captures how the sample behaves/appears\n  (distinct from disease diagnosis).\n\nterm: treatment\nsdrf_column: characteristics[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Treatment or perturbation applied to the sample (drug, stimulus,\n  environmental stress, growth condition change).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable. Examples: "doxycycline\n  treatment", "heat shock", "DMSO vehicle control".\n\nterm: compound\nsdrf_column: characteristics[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:24431, CHEBI, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Chemical compound or drug applied to the sample.\ninstructions: Use ChEBI if possible. Return null if no compound treatment. Do NOT\n  include the crosslinking reagent here — that goes in comment[cross-linker].\n\nterm: dose\nsdrf_column: characteristics[dose]\nontology: NCIT:C25488\ndescription: Dose or concentration of compound treatment.\npattern: number + unit (e.g. 10 mg/kg, 50 uM, 100 nM, 10 mg/mL)\ninstructions: Return null if no compound treatment or dose not stated.\n\nterm: genetic modification\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genetic modification]\nontology: EFO:0000510, EFO\ndescription: Method of genetic modification (knockout, knockdown, overexpression,\n  transduction). Describes HOW the modification was done.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: perturbation\nsdrf_column: characteristics[perturbation]\nontology: PRIDE:0000700\ndescription: Type of perturbation applied (environmental/experimental perturbation).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: depletion\nsdrf_column: characteristics[depletion]\nontology: PRIDE:0000020\ndescription: Whether abundant protein depletion was performed.\nallowed_values: no depletion, depletion, not applicable\ninstructions: Return "no depletion" if not mentioned. Return "depletion" if\n  high-abundance protein removal is described (e.g. albumin depletion).\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nStructural mechanisms of  autoinhibition and substrate recognition  by the ubiquitin ligase HACE1\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nUbiquitin ligases (E3s) are pivotal specificity determinants in the ubiquitin system by selecting substrates and decorating them with distinct ubiquitin signals. Structure determination of the underlying, specific E3-substrate complexes, however, has proven challenging due to their transient nature. In particular, it is incompletely understood how members of the catalytic cysteine-driven class of HECT-type ligases position substrate proteins for modification. Here we report a cryo-EM structure of the full-length human HECT-type ligase HACE1, along with solution-based conformational analyses by small-angle X-ray scattering and hydrogen-deuterium exchange mass spectrometry. Structure-based functional analyses in vitro and in cells reveal that the activity of HACE1 is stringently regulated by dimerization-induced autoinhibition. The inhibition occurs at the first step of the catalytic cycle and is thus substrate-independent. We employ mechanism-based chemical crosslinking to reconstitute a complex of activated, monomeric HACE1 with its major substrate, RAC1, visualize its structure by cryo-EM, and validate the binding mode by solution-based analyses. Our findings explain how HACE1 achieves selectivity in ubiquitinating the active, GTP-loaded state of RAC1 and establish a framework for interpreting mutational alterations of the HACE1-RAC1 interplay in disease. More broadly, this work illuminates central unexplored aspects in the architecture, conformational dynamics, regulation, and specificity of full-length HECT-type ligases.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor HDX-MS analyses of HACE1 apo, samples contained 50 µM protein (Dataset S1). Samples for the analysis of HACE1 ΔN-RAC1 Q61L interactions contained either 50 µM RAC1 +/- 100 µM HACE1 or 50 µM HACE1 +/- 100 µM RAC1 (Dataset S2). For the corresponding analyses of HACE1 S14E or I694D, samples contained 25 µM of HACE1 +/- 50 µM RAC1 (Dataset S3). All samples were prepared by a two-arm autosampler (LEAP Technologies). Reactions were initiated by adding 58.5 µL of a D2O-based buffer (Dataset S1: 20 mM HEPES (pH 8.0), 150 mM NaCl, 5 mM DTT; Datasets S2 and S3: 20 mM HEPES (pH 7.8), 100 mM NaCl, 1 mM MgCl2, 5 mM DTT) to 6.5 µL of protein solution. After incubation at 25 °C for the indicated time points, HDX was stopped by mixing 55 µL of the reaction with 55 µL of 400 mM KH2PO4/H3PO4, 2 M guanidine-HCl (pH 2.2) at 1 °C. 100 μL of the quenched reaction was injected into an ACQUITY UPLC M-Class system with HDX Technology. Non-deuterated samples were generated analogously with an H2O-based buffer. The samples were washed from a 50-μL injection loop with water + 0.1% (v/v) formic acid (100 μL/min) into an immobilized porcine pepsin-loaded column at 12 °C. Peptides were collected on a trap column (2 mm x 2 cm; 0.5 °C) filled with POROS 20 R2 (Thermo Fisher Scientific). After 3 min, the column was placed in line with an ACQUITY UPLC BEH C18 1.7 μm 1.0 x 100 mm column (Waters) at 0.5 °C and the peptides were eluted with a gradient of water + 0.1% (v/v) formic acid (eluent A) and acetonitrile + 0.1% (v/v) formic acid (eluent B) at 30 μL/min, as follows: 0-7 min/95-65% A, 7-8 min/65-15% A, 8-10 min/15% A. The peptides were guided to a Synapt G2-Si mass spectrometer (Waters) and ionized by electrospray ionization (capillary temperature: 250 °C; spray voltage: 3.0 kV). Mass spectra were acquired with MassLynX MS 4.1 (Waters) over 50 to 2,000 m/z in enhanced high-definition MS (HDMSE) or high-definition MS (HDMS) mode for non-deuterated and deuterated samples, respectively. Lock-mass correction was conducted with [Glu1]-fibrinopeptide B standard (Waters). During peptide separation on the ACQUITY UPLC BEH C18 column, the pepsin column was washed 3 times with 80 μL of 0.5 M guanidine-HCl in 4% (v/v) acetonitrile. Blank runs (double-distilled H2O) were performed between each sample. All measurements were performed in triplicates (separate HDX reactions).\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
